 In the realm of Adobe JavaScript, you can use the `textFlow` and `ColumnText` classes to force a textfield to display columns. Here's a basic example:

1. First, ensure that the textfield is editable and has a `textFlow` property. You can do this in the Adobe UI by selecting the textfield and going to "Object > Text > Text Options > Text Field Properties > Text Flow".

2. Next, create a new `TextFlow` object and set its `alignment` property to `TextAlignment.JUSTIFY_ALL` to justify the text across multiple columns.

3. Add the text you want to display to the `TextFlow` object.

4. Create a new `ColumnText` object and set its `width` and `numVoices` properties to control the number and width of columns.

5. Set the `textFlow` property of the `ColumnText` object to the `TextFlow` object you created earlier.

6. Finally, call the `showTextAtFrame` method of the `ColumnText` object, passing in the textfield as the destination frame.

Here's a code snippet that demonstrates this process:

```javascript
var textField = activeDocument.textLayers.item(0);
var textFlow = new TextFlow();
textFlow.alignment = TextAlignment.JUSTIFY_ALL;
textFlow.addText("Your text here...");

var columnText = new ColumnText();
columnText.width = 500; // Set the width of the columns
columnText.numVoices = 2; // Set the number of columns
columnText.textFlow = textFlow;

columnText.showTextAtFrame(textField);
```

This code will display the text in the `textFlow` object across two columns in the specified textfield. You can adjust the `width` and `numVoices` properties of the `ColumnText` object to control the number and width of columns as needed.

